mod('replication','DNA replication',
 'Before a cell divides it copies every base of its DNA. The copy is made by a machine that reads each old strand and builds a new partner.',
 ['Replication is <b>semiconservative</b>: each new DNA molecule has one old strand and one new strand. It starts at an <b>origin of replication</b>. <b>Helicase</b> unwinds the double helix, making a replication fork; single-strand binding proteins keep the strands apart; <b>topoisomerase</b> relieves twisting ahead of the fork. <b>Primase</b> makes a short RNA <b>primer</b> because DNA polymerase can only add nucleotides to an existing 3′ end. <b>DNA polymerase</b> then adds nucleotides only in the <b>5′ → 3′</b> direction, reading the template 3′ → 5′.',
  'Because the strands are antiparallel, one new strand (the <b>leading strand</b>) is made continuously toward the fork. The other (the <b>lagging strand</b>) is made away from the fork in short pieces called <b>Okazaki fragments</b>, each needing its own primer. <b>DNA ligase</b> joins the fragments after the primers are replaced with DNA. DNA polymerase also proofreads, so errors are rare (about 1 in 10⁹–10¹⁰ bases after repair).'],
 ('DNA copying was just "unzip and match up"','that the strands are antiparallel and polymerase only works 5′ → 3′, so one strand is made in pieces','why replication needs primers and ligase and why it is so accurate','semiconservative replication and the enzymes of the fork'),
 [('new strand is made 5′ → 3′','DNA polymerase'),('helicase unwinds; primase starts; ligase joins','fork enzymes'),('leading strand: continuous','lagging: Okazaki fragments'),('semiconservative: each daughter = 1 old + 1 new strand','Meselson–Stahl')],
 [stagelab('rp-lab','The replication fork',canvas='rp-canvas',aria='A replication fork with helicase, primers, DNA polymerase, Okazaki fragments and ligase appearing in successive stages.',p='rp',n=5),
  lab('rp-lab2','The Meselson–Stahl experiment',canvas='rp-canvas2',aria='Bars showing the fraction of DNA molecules that are heavy, hybrid or light after each generation of growth in light nitrogen.',
   ctl=[('rp-g','Generations in ¹⁴N',0,6,1,2)],
   out=[('rp-tot','DNA molecules from one original','x'),('rp-hyb','hybrid (¹⁵N/¹⁴N)','a'),('rp-lt','light (¹⁴N/¹⁴N)','v'),('rp-hv','heavy (¹⁵N/¹⁵N)','')],own=True)],
 [('Go to the stage with Okazaki fragments. Why does only one strand have them? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#rp-st",3]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','DNA polymerase can only build 5′ → 3′. On one template that direction points toward the fork (continuous); on the other it points away, so the strand is built in pieces as the fork opens.'),
  ('Set 2 generations. What fraction are hybrid? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#rp-g",2]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','2 of 4 molecules (50%) are hybrid and 2 of 4 are light. No heavy DNA remains, and this matches semiconservative replication.')],
 ('Bacteria grown for many generations in ¹⁵N are moved to ¹⁴N and allowed to divide three times. What fraction of DNA molecules are hybrid and what fraction light?',[
  '<b>Molecules.</b> Each generation doubles the number: 2³ = 8 molecules from one.',
  '<b>Old strands.</b> The two original ¹⁵N strands end up in 2 different molecules, each a hybrid (one old, one new).',
  '<b>Fractions.</b> Hybrid = 2/8 = 1/4; light = 6/8 = 3/4; heavy = 0.']),
 ['Writing that DNA polymerase works 3′ → 5′ (it adds to a 3′ end, building 5′ → 3′).','Thinking primers are DNA. They are short RNA.','Saying both strands are made in fragments.','Forgetting that the old strands are conserved (one in each daughter).','Confusing ligase (joins fragments) with polymerase.'],
 ('Predicting results',['If primase is missing, polymerase cannot start. If ligase is missing, the lagging strand has gaps. If helicase fails, the fork does not open. State which strand is affected and why.']),
 [('Okazaki fragments are made because DNA polymerase',['can only add nucleotides in the 5′ → 3′ direction','cannot copy RNA','works only at the end of chromosomes','needs ATP to unwind DNA'],0,'On the lagging strand the 5′ → 3′ direction points away from the fork, so synthesis is in pieces.')])

mod('transcription','Transcription & RNA processing',
 'DNA stays in the nucleus; a working copy of one gene travels out. That copy is made of RNA.',
 ['<b>Transcription</b> makes an RNA copy of a gene. <b>RNA polymerase</b> binds to the <b>promoter</b>, unwinds a short stretch of DNA and builds an RNA strand 5′ → 3′ by pairing RNA nucleotides with the <b>template strand</b> (A–U, T–A, G–C, C–G). No primer is needed. The RNA is complementary to the template and has the same sequence as the coding strand, with U in place of T. Transcription ends at a terminator sequence.',
  'In prokaryotes the RNA is used immediately. In eukaryotes the primary transcript is processed before leaving the nucleus: a <b>5′ cap</b> and a <b>poly-A tail</b> are added, and <b>introns</b> are removed while <b>exons</b> are joined (<b>splicing</b>) to make mature mRNA. <b>Alternative splicing</b> lets one gene make several proteins. Other RNAs (tRNA, rRNA) are also made by transcription and are not translated.'],
 ('DNA directly made proteins','that RNA is an intermediate copy and that eukaryotic RNA is edited','why mRNA contains U and why splicing matters','promoter, template strand, RNA polymerase and splicing'),
 [('RNA is made 5′ → 3′','RNA polymerase, no primer'),('pairs: A–U, T–A, G–C, C–G','template → mRNA'),('5′ cap + poly-A tail + splicing','eukaryotic mRNA processing'),('mRNA sequence = coding strand with U for T','same as non-template')],
 [stagelab('tx-lab','Making mRNA',canvas='tx-canvas',aria='RNA polymerase moving along DNA, building mRNA, then eukaryotic processing removing introns and adding a cap and tail.',p='tx',n=4),
  lab('tx-lab2','Transcribe a template',canvas='tx-canvas2',aria='The template strand of DNA, the mRNA built from it and the codons.',
   pre='<div class="ctl"><label for="tx-in"><span>Template DNA, written 3′ → 5′ (A, T, G, C)</span></label><input type="text" id="tx-in" value="TACCGACTTTTCAAACCTATT" maxlength="36" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" style="font-family:var(--mono);width:100%;padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--surface);color:var(--ink)"></div>',
   out=[('tx-mrna','mRNA (5′ → 3′)','x'),('tx-cod','codons','a'),('tx-len','length','v')],own=True)],
 [('Go to the processing stage. What is removed and what is added? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#tx-st",3]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','Introns are removed and exons joined (splicing); a 5′ cap and a poly-A tail are added.'),
  ('The template reads 3′-TACGGATTC-5′. What is the mRNA? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#tx-in","TACGGATTC"]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','5′-AUGCCUAAG-3′: each base is replaced by its RNA partner and the strand runs the other way.')],
 ('A template strand reads 3′-TACCGATTT-5′. Write the mRNA and the coding strand.',[
  '<b>Pair bases.</b> T→A, A→U, C→G, C→G, G→C, A→U, T→A, T→A, T→A: AUGGCUAAA.',
  '<b>mRNA.</b> 5′-AUGGCUAAA-3′.',
  '<b>Coding strand.</b> It has the same sequence with T for U: 5′-ATGGCTAAA-3′.']),
 ['Writing T in mRNA.','Copying the template instead of its complement.','Reading the template in the wrong direction (the RNA is built 5′ → 3′, antiparallel to the template).','Saying introns are translated.','Confusing the template strand with the coding strand.'],
 ('Common question types',['Given a template or coding strand, write the mRNA and then the peptide. Given a mutation in the promoter or a splice site, predict the effect: no transcription, or the wrong exons joined.']),
 [('Which sequence is the mRNA made from the DNA template 3′-TAC-5′?',['5′-AUG-3′','5′-ATG-3′','5′-UAC-3′','5′-TAC-3′'],0,'Complement with U in place of T: AUG.')])

mod('translation','Translation & the genetic code',
 'Ribosomes read the mRNA three bases at a time and string together amino acids in the order the codons specify.',
 ['The <b>genetic code</b> is read in <b>codons</b> of three bases. There are 64 codons: 61 code for amino acids and 3 are <b>stop</b> codons (UAA, UAG, UGA). <b>AUG</b> codes for methionine and is the <b>start</b> codon. The code is <b>redundant</b> (several codons for one amino acid) but <b>unambiguous</b> (each codon codes for only one), and it is nearly universal.',
  'The <b>ribosome</b> (rRNA and protein) binds mRNA. Each <b>tRNA</b> carries one amino acid and has an <b>anticodon</b> complementary to a codon. At the start, a tRNA with methionine pairs with AUG in the P site. Then a tRNA enters the A site, a peptide bond forms, and the ribosome moves one codon (translocation). This repeats until a stop codon reaches the A site and release factors free the polypeptide.'],
 ('proteins were just "made from DNA"','how the sequence of codons becomes the sequence of amino acids','why one change in a base may or may not change the protein','codons, tRNA anticodons and the stages of translation'),
 [('codon: three mRNA bases','AUG = start (Met); UAA, UAG, UGA = stop'),('anticodon is complementary to the codon','on tRNA'),('amino acids in peptide = (nucleotides before stop) / 3','coding region'),('code: redundant, unambiguous, universal','properties')],
 [stagelab('tl-lab','Building a polypeptide',canvas='tl-canvas',aria='A ribosome on an mRNA: initiation with Met-tRNA, elongation with a new tRNA and a peptide bond, translocation, and termination at a stop codon.',p='tl',n=5),
  lab('tl-lab2','Translate an mRNA',canvas='tl-canvas2',aria='Codons of the mRNA coloured by the amino acid they code for, from the start codon to a stop codon.',
   pre='<div class="ctl"><label for="tl-in"><span>mRNA, written 5′ → 3′ (A, U, G, C)</span></label><input type="text" id="tl-in" value="GCAUGGCUGAAAAGUUUGGAUAAGC" maxlength="48" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" style="font-family:var(--mono);width:100%;padding:8px;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;background:var(--surface);color:var(--ink)"></div>',
   out=[('tl-pep','polypeptide','x'),('tl-n','amino acids','a'),('tl-note','note','')],own=True)],
 [('Go to the stage where the peptide bond forms. Which molecules join? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#tl-st",2]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','The amino acid on the tRNA in the P site is joined to the one on the tRNA in the A site (the ribosome catalyzes it).'),
  ('Change the mRNA so the first AUG is missing. What happens? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#tl-in","GCAGGCUGAAAAGUUUGGAUAAGC"]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','No start codon: translation does not begin (the tool reports no peptide).')],
 ('The mRNA 5′-AUGUUUGGCAAAUAG-3′ is translated. What is the polypeptide and how many amino acids does it have?',[
  '<b>Codons.</b> AUG | UUU | GGC | AAA | UAG.',
  '<b>Amino acids.</b> AUG = Met, UUU = Phe, GGC = Gly, AAA = Lys, UAG = stop.',
  '<b>Answer.</b> Met–Phe–Gly–Lys: 4 amino acids. Stop codons do not code for an amino acid.']),
 ['Counting the stop codon as an amino acid.','Starting to read at the first base instead of the first AUG.','Writing anticodons as identical to codons.','Saying the code is ambiguous (it is redundant, not ambiguous).','Mixing up tRNA (anticodon) with mRNA (codon).'],
 ('Using the code table',['Use the genetic code table on the Reference tab: first base on the left, second on top, third on the right. If given DNA, first make mRNA. For a tRNA anticodon, complement the codon (and remember the strands are antiparallel).']),
 [('A tRNA has the anticodon 3′-UAC-5′. Which codon does it pair with?',['5′-AUG-3′','5′-UAC-3′','5′-UUU-3′','5′-ATG-3′'],0,'The codon is the complement: 5′-AUG-3′, the start codon.')])

mod('generegs','Regulation of gene expression',
 'Every cell has the same genes but uses different ones. Cells switch genes on and off depending on conditions and identity.',
 ['In bacteria, genes with related functions are grouped in an <b>operon</b> with one promoter and an <b>operator</b>. In the <b>lac operon</b> (inducible), a repressor normally blocks the operator so the genes for lactose digestion are off. If <b>lactose</b> is present, a derivative binds the repressor and releases it, and transcription can happen. When glucose is also present the CAP–cAMP activator is not active, so transcription is low even with lactose. In the <b>trp operon</b> (repressible), tryptophan binds the repressor and turns it on, so the genes are switched off when tryptophan is plentiful.',
  'In eukaryotes there are more control points: <b>chromatin</b> structure (acetylation of histones loosens DNA; DNA methylation silences genes: epigenetics), <b>transcription factors</b> binding <b>enhancers</b> and promoters, <b>alternative splicing</b>, mRNA stability and <b>microRNAs</b> (RNA interference), and control of translation and protein breakdown. This is how identical DNA yields different cell types.'],
 ('genes were simply "on"','that cells actively switch genes on and off using regulatory proteins and DNA sequences','how cells respond to their environment and become different types','operons, transcription factors, chromatin and RNA control'),
 [('lac operon: lactose present → repressor off → transcription','inducible'),('high glucose → low cAMP → little CAP activation','even with lactose'),('trp operon: tryptophan binds repressor → genes off','repressible'),('acetylation opens chromatin; methylation silences genes','eukaryotic control')],
 [lab('gr-lab','The lac operon',canvas='gr-canvas',aria='The lac operon: promoter, operator and three genes, with the repressor bound or released and RNA polymerase transcribing or not depending on lactose and glucose.',
   seg=('gr-lac','Lactose',[('no','absent'),('yes','present')]),
   pre='<div class="row"><button type="button" class="btn" id="gr-glu" aria-pressed="true">Glucose: present</button></div>',
   out=[('gr-rep','repressor','x'),('gr-cap','CAP–cAMP activator','a'),('gr-out','transcription of lac genes','v')],own=True),
  lab('gr-lab2','The trp operon',canvas='gr-canvas2',aria='The trp operon with the repressor active or inactive depending on whether tryptophan is present.',
   seg=('gr-trp','Tryptophan',[('lo','low'),('hi','high')]),
   out=[('gr-trep','repressor','x'),('gr-tout','transcription of trp genes','a')],own=True)],
 [('Lactose present and glucose absent. What happens? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#gr-lac [data-k=yes]"],["click","#gr-glu"]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','The repressor is released and the CAP–cAMP activator is active: high transcription of the lac genes.'),
  ('Now add glucose back with lactose present. <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#gr-glu"]) + '\'>Toggle glucose</button>','','Transcription drops: cAMP is low so CAP does not activate, even though the repressor is off. The cell prefers glucose.'),
  ('Set tryptophan high. Why are the trp genes off? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#gr-trp [data-k=hi]"]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','Tryptophan binds the repressor and activates it, so it blocks the operator: no need to make more tryptophan.')],
 ('E. coli has a mutation so that the lac repressor cannot bind lactose (but still binds the operator). Predict lac gene expression with and without lactose.',[
  '<b>Normal repressor.</b> Releases from the operator when lactose is present.',
  '<b>Mutant.</b> It cannot respond to lactose, so it stays on the operator.',
  '<b>Result.</b> The genes stay off with or without lactose. The cell cannot digest lactose.']),
 ['Saying lactose binds the operator (it binds the repressor, via a derivative).','Mixing up inducible (lac, normally off) and repressible (trp, normally on) operons.','Saying operons are found in eukaryotes (they are typical of prokaryotes).','Assuming methylation always turns genes on (it usually silences them).','Forgetting that every cell has the same DNA; the difference is in gene expression.'],
 ('Prediction template',['State the signal (lactose, glucose, tryptophan), what it does to the regulatory protein, whether RNA polymerase can transcribe, and the effect on the proteins made. Mutation questions: ask which part (repressor, operator, promoter) no longer works.']),
 [('In the lac operon, lactose causes transcription by',['binding the repressor so it leaves the operator','binding RNA polymerase','binding the promoter','destroying the operator'],0,'The inducer releases the repressor from the operator.')])

mod('mutation','Mutations',
 'A change in the DNA sequence may change nothing, make a protein slightly different, or ruin it completely. What happens depends on the kind of change and where it occurs.',
 ['A <b>point mutation</b> changes one base. A <b>silent</b> mutation changes the codon but not the amino acid (the code is redundant). A <b>missense</b> mutation changes one amino acid (sickle cell: GAG → GUG, glutamic acid → valine). A <b>nonsense</b> mutation turns an amino acid codon into a stop codon, so the protein is cut short. An <b>insertion</b> or <b>deletion</b> of bases that is not a multiple of three causes a <b>frameshift</b>: every codon after it is read differently, usually ruining the protein. Insertions or deletions of multiples of three add or remove amino acids without shifting the frame.',
  'Mutations arise from copying errors, <b>mutagens</b> (UV, radiation, chemicals) and mistakes in repair. They create new alleles, the raw material for evolution. Only mutations in germ cells (gametes) are passed to offspring; somatic mutations can cause cancer. Many mutations are neutral, some harmful, and a few beneficial depending on the environment.'],
 ('mutations were always harmful','that the effect depends on the type of change and its location','why one base change can be harmless and another disastrous','silent, missense, nonsense and frameshift mutations'),
 [('silent: same amino acid','missense: different amino acid'),('nonsense: becomes a stop codon','shortened protein'),('indel not a multiple of 3: frameshift','all later codons change'),('only germ-line mutations are inherited','somatic: not passed on')],
 [lab('mt-lab','Mutate a gene',canvas='mt-canvas',aria='Codons of a gene before and after the chosen mutation with the resulting amino acids. Changed codons are highlighted.',
   seg=('mt-kind','Mutation',[('none','none'),('sil','silent'),('mis','missense'),('non','nonsense'),('ins','insert 1 base'),('del','delete 1 base'),('d3','delete 3 bases')]),
   out=[('mt-type','type of change','x'),('mt-prot','protein','a'),('mt-eff','effect','')],own=True)],
 [('Choose missense. Which codon changed and what is the result? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#mt-kind [data-k=mis]"]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','GAA → GUA changes Glu to Val: one amino acid differs, the rest of the protein is the same.'),
  ('Choose "insert 1 base". Compare with "delete 3 bases". <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#mt-kind [data-k=ins]"]) + '\'>Insert 1</button>','','One base shifts the reading frame so all later codons change (frameshift). Deleting exactly three bases removes one amino acid and leaves the frame intact.')],
 ('The coding strand reads 5′-ATG GCT GAA AAG TTT GGA TAA-3′. A mutation changes AAG to TAG. Name the mutation and predict the protein.',[
  '<b>Original.</b> Met–Ala–Glu–Lys–Phe–Gly (stop).',
  '<b>Change.</b> AAG → TAG: in mRNA AAG (Lys) becomes UAG, a stop codon.',
  '<b>Type and effect.</b> Nonsense. The protein is Met–Ala–Glu (3 amino acids), likely nonfunctional.']),
 ['Calling all mutations harmful.','Saying a silent mutation does not change the DNA (it does, but not the amino acid).','Forgetting that a frameshift needs an insertion or deletion not divisible by 3.','Thinking somatic mutations are inherited.','Mixing nonsense (stop) with missense (different amino acid).'],
 ('Classify by comparing',['Translate the original and the mutant side by side. Same amino acids: silent. One different: missense. Stop appears early: nonsense. Many different after a point: frameshift. Remember the effect on function depends on where the change is.']),
 [('A base change turns a codon for glutamic acid into a codon for valine. This is a',['missense mutation','silent mutation','nonsense mutation','frameshift mutation'],0,'One amino acid is replaced by a different one.')])

mod('biotech','Biotechnology & genetic engineering',
 'Biologists can copy, cut, sort, read and rewrite DNA. Each technique rests on a simple property of DNA.',
 ['<b>PCR</b> (polymerase chain reaction) copies a target region. A cycle has three steps: <b>denature</b> (about 95 °C, strands separate), <b>anneal</b> (about 55 °C, primers bind) and <b>extend</b> (72 °C, a heat-stable polymerase such as Taq builds new strands). Each cycle doubles the target: after n cycles there are about 2<sup>n</sup> copies. <b>Restriction enzymes</b> cut DNA at specific palindromic sequences. <b>Gel electrophoresis</b> separates DNA fragments by size: DNA is negatively charged and moves toward the positive electrode, and smaller fragments travel farther.',
  '<b>Recombinant DNA</b>: a gene and a plasmid are cut with the same restriction enzyme, joined with <b>ligase</b>, and the plasmid is put into bacteria (<b>transformation</b>); a selectable marker such as antibiotic resistance finds the bacteria that took it up. <b>CRISPR-Cas9</b> uses a guide RNA to direct the Cas9 enzyme to a matching DNA sequence, where it cuts so the gene can be disrupted or edited. DNA sequencing reads the order of bases. These tools underlie GMOs, gene therapy, forensics and diagnostics.'],
 ('DNA was too small or fragile to handle','that DNA\'s charge, complementary pairing and recognizable sequences make it easy to copy, cut and sort','how scientists amplify, analyze and edit DNA','PCR, restriction digests, gel electrophoresis, plasmids and CRISPR'),
 [('copies after n PCR cycles ≈ 2ⁿ','ideal doubling'),('PCR: 95 °C denature, ~55 °C anneal, 72 °C extend','Taq polymerase'),('gel: DNA moves toward + ; smaller = farther','electrophoresis'),('restriction enzyme + ligase → recombinant plasmid','cloning')],
 [lab('bt-lab','PCR cycles',canvas='bt-canvas',aria='A temperature cycle and a bar showing the number of copies of the target after the chosen number of cycles.',
   ctl=[('bt-n','Cycles',0,30,1,10)],
   out=[('bt-c','copies of the target','x'),('bt-t','time (about 2 minutes per cycle)','a')],own=True),
  lab('bt-lab2','Gel electrophoresis of a restriction digest',canvas='bt-canvas2',aria='A gel with lanes for a size ladder and the DNA cut by the chosen restriction enzymes, with fragments shown as bands.',
   seg=('bt-enz','Digest a 6,000 bp linear DNA with',[('none','nothing'),('E','enzyme E (cuts at 1,500 and 4,000)'),('B','enzyme B (cuts at 2,400)'),('EB','both E and B')]),
   out=[('bt-fr','fragment sizes (bp)','x'),('bt-n2','number of fragments','a')],own=True)],
 [('Set 10 cycles. How many copies of the target? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["#bt-n",10]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','2¹⁰ = 1,024 copies from one starting molecule (in the ideal case).'),
  ('Digest with enzyme E. Which band travels farthest? <button type="button" class="btn link" data-show=\'' + show(["click","#bt-enz [data-k=E]"]) + '\'>Set it up for me</button>','','The smallest fragment (1,500 bp) travels farthest because it moves through the gel mesh most easily.')],
 ('A 6,000 bp linear DNA is cut by enzyme E at positions 1,500 and 4,000. What fragments appear, and which is nearest the wells?',[
  '<b>Cuts.</b> At 1,500 and 4,000: three fragments.',
  '<b>Sizes.</b> 1,500; 4,000 − 1,500 = 2,500; 6,000 − 4,000 = 2,000 bp (sum 6,000).',
  '<b>Gel.</b> The largest (2,500 bp) moves the least, so it is nearest the wells; the 1,500 bp fragment moves farthest.']),
 ['Saying DNA moves toward the negative electrode (it is negatively charged, so it moves to the positive one).','Thinking larger fragments move farther.','Forgetting that PCR needs primers and a heat-stable polymerase.','Saying every bacterium takes up the plasmid (that is why selection is needed).','Believing CRISPR uses protein alone to find the target (the guide RNA does the matching).'],
 ('Interpreting gels',['Compare band positions with the ladder: farther from the wells means smaller. Add up fragment sizes to check they equal the total DNA length. For comparisons (such as a mutation destroying a restriction site), predict how the pattern of bands changes.']),
 [('In gel electrophoresis, the DNA fragments that travel the farthest are the',['smallest','largest','most positively charged','those with the most guanine'],0,'Smaller fragments move through the gel matrix faster.')])
